import{_ as t,o,c as a,an as d}from"./chunks/framework.UzZ94frR.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Model protocol","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"protocol.md","filePath":"protocol.md","lastUpdated":null}'),i={name:"protocol.md"};function l(n,e,s,c,r,p){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[d('<h1 id="Model-protocol" tabindex="-1">Model protocol <a class="header-anchor" href="#Model-protocol" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Model protocol {#Model-protocol}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A model is any Julia value that supplies a conditional observation likelihood. NLMEEstimation supplies the subject-level random-effect prior, <code>ηᵢ ~ N(0, Ω)</code>, and represents Ω by blockwise log-Cholesky coordinates. The model supplies θ and σ in its own unconstrained coordinates.</p><p>Every model implements <code>nsubjects(model)</code> and <code>nlme_layout(model)</code>. It can use either evaluation style:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Style</th><th style="text-align:right;">Required conditional likelihood</th><th style="text-align:right;">How estimators evaluate it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>PerSubjectEvaluation()</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>conditional_loglikelihood(model, i, θ, σ, η)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">The package gathers subject calls into a batch.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>BatchedEvaluation()</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>conditional_loglikelihoods(model, subjects, θ, σ, H)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">One call evaluates the listed subjects; column <code>k</code> of <code>H</code> is that subject&#39;s η.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The likelihood excludes the η prior. Derivatives may be provided directly or obtained through <code>ad_backend(model)</code> with DifferentiationInterface. Reverse-mode Enzyme is the tested backend. Per-subject η Hessian blocks are optional; set <code>hessian_ad_backend(model) = nothing</code> for a gradient-only model.</p><p>FOCE and FOCEI require <code>observation_model(model) = GaussianObservations()</code> and observation means, variances, and first η Jacobians. SAEM can use <code>mu_referencing(model)</code> for a closed-form update of the corresponding fixed effects and Ω; remaining population coordinates use a numerical M-step.</p><p>A batched model may keep its batched likelihood and η derivatives while using subject-level population gradients in SAEM. Implement <code>conditional_loglikelihood_and_population_gradient(model, i, θ, σ, η)</code> and declare <code>saem_population_gradient_style(model) = PerSubjectEvaluation()</code>. SAEM then sums those subject values and gradients for the numerical M-step. This choice does not change <code>evaluation_style(model)</code> or any other estimator step.</p><p>Run <code>check_protocol(model, θ, σ, ω)</code> while implementing a model. It checks dimensions, agreement between evaluation styles, subject separability, available derivatives, and declared observation and mu-referencing traits.</p><p>The <a href="./pk-comparison">PK comparison</a> shows a complete batched BRM+RKPPL model and the calls used to fit it. The <a href="./api">API reference</a> lists every protocol entry point.</p>',9)])])}const u=t(i,[["render",l]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
